sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch04-03> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The nucleus is almost always spherical because its fluid substance does not mix with the surrounding protoplasm, so it is bounded by a film of uniform surface tension and immersed in a medium transmitting uniform fluid pressure; where the cytoplasm becomes more solid (as in muscle cells or mucus-laden epithelial cells) the pressure becomes unsymmetrical and the nucleus is deformed.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The nucleus is spherical because it is an immiscible fluid drop under uniform tension";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IV, “On the Internal Form and Structure of the Cell”; topic “Form of the resting nucleus”. Scope: nuclei of cells generally; contrasted with muscle and epithelial cells with stiffened cytoplasm"
}
